NOTE: You are planning, not implementing. The codebase may not contain the directories
listed in SCOPE yet. Do NOT explore implementation directories that don't exist —
plan from the provided spec artifacts (architecture document, goal spec, or assigned scope).
PLANNING BOUNDARY:
If DESCRIPTION, DONE WHEN, SCOPE, or validation names implementation, documentation,
configuration, test, or support files, treat those as downstream child-task scope.
As code-planner, do not modify those files. Encode the required changes in the plan
file and output[] entries only.
{{- if .RCARequired}}
ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (REQUIRED — this objective is a defect fix):
The plan file MUST contain "## Root Cause Analysis" before the task breakdown:
 a. Evidence — source and code-path claims cite file:line read this session.
    Runtime claims (an observed failure, a crash, a reported state) cite a
    checkable artifact instead: the exact command and its output, a log excerpt,
    or explicit attribution to the reporter. Nothing from memory.
 b. Mechanism — the code path that produces the failure, not its category.
 c. Reproduction — the concrete state that triggers it. At least one output[]
    done_when must reference it, so the coder's failing test hits the real path.
 d. Verification scope — what you checked and what you did not. Unchecked claims
    are marked unverified or reporter-attributed, never stated flat.
 e. Sufficiency — does fixing this cause make the failure impossible? If a residual
    path remains, name it.
 f. Adjacent findings — defects found outside scope: recorded, NOT planned.
If the spec already states a cause, verify it against source and cite — do not
restate. If no cause can be established from evidence, mark BLOCKED rather than
planning against a hypothesis.
